{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent level bias: forecasts are generally too low during sustained uptrends (examples 1–4, early horizons). Apply a +0.5 to +1.5 €/t upward bias correction when the last 5–10 days show a positive slope.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: error tends to grow with horizon; mid-horizon (≈day 8–20) is often most underpredicted in rising regimes. Use a ramped bias: +0.3 €/t at day 1 increasing linearly to about +1.2 €/t by day 15, then taper.",
    "Overreaction/overshoot after local peaks: when the model projects continued strength into the far horizon, realized prices revert lower (example 3 late horizon; example 5 mid/late). Cap forecast momentum after a recent spike: damp projected daily changes by ~30–50% beyond day 10 if the last 3–5 days include a sharp rise.",
    "Mean-reversion underestimation: the model often keeps prices elevated too long after a run-up; true outcomes revert toward the recent 10–20d mean faster. Blend forecasts toward the 15d moving average with increasing weight over horizon (e.g., 10% at day 5 → 30% at day 15 → 40% at day 30) when recent volatility is high.",
    "Turning-point lag: forecasts miss inflection points—too bearish early in rallies, too bullish late in rallies. Add a regime switch heuristic: if price is above the 20d MA and MA slope is rising, bias up; if price is >1.5σ above 20d MA, bias down and increase mean-reversion weight."
  ]
}
